<h1 style="font-size: 2.25em; font-weight: 700; line-height: 1.2; margin-bottom: 24px; color: #000000;">What to Do a Summer in Galicia: 3 Routes to Choose According to Your Style</h1>

We are always haunted by the same cliché: that it rains all year round in Galicia, that summer here is just a three-day mirage. But those of us who live here know the truth. When the heat turns up, this land turns into a kind of à la carte paradise that we might well baptize as **Galifornia**: white sand beaches that look like the Caribbean, sunsets that stay etched in your memory, and a light that holds out until ten at night. The difference is that here, instead of avocado, there is <span style="color: #FF7F50; font-weight: bold;">pulpo á feira</span>, and instead of waves and Californian surfers, there are mussel rafts (*bateas*) looming in every estuary.

The problem isn't a lack of plans in Galicia — the problem is that Galicia is infinite, and trying to cover it all in a few days is an impossible mission. So you don't go crazy trying to figure out an impossible map, we have prepared three routes with three totally different souls: <span style="color: #FF7F50; font-weight: bold;">islands and rias</span>, <span style="color: #FF7F50; font-weight: bold;">wild coast with lighthouses</span>, or <span style="color: #FF7F50; font-weight: bold;">inland vineyards</span>. Choose yours — and if you prefer [<span style="color: #FF7F50; text-decoration: underline; font-weight: bold;">MARCO</span>](https://marcotravel.io) to help you turn it into an itinerary, you can [generate your personalized Galicia itinerary in minutes](https://marcotravel.io).

## Plan 1: A weekend of islands, hórreos, and the best dunset of your life

This is a way to enjoy a weekend in the **Rías Baixas** without complications: a day on an island, a second day between Pontevedra and Combarro, and a sunset in Bueu that caps off the trip in style.

### <span style="color: #FF7F50; font-weight: bold;">Day 1 — Cíes or Ons</span>

Both islands are part of the **Atlantic Islands National Park**, and that implies an important condition to keep in mind: between May 15 and September 15, access is limited by law, with a daily quota of visitors and mandatory authorization in addition to the boat ticket. It’s not a recommendation, it’s a requirement — in the middle of August, it is best to request authorization on the [official website of the Xunta de Galicia](https://autorizacionillasatlanticas.xunta.gal) at least a week in advance. 

The Cíes Islands can be reached from Vigo, Cangas, or Baiona; Ons from Bueu, Portonovo, or Sanxenxo, with a crossing of about 40 minutes and fewer crowds than its older sister. Whatever your choice, dedicate the full day to it: beach, trail, and a leisurely stroll, with no other plans on the agenda.

If you depart from Vigo, take the opportunity to explore **O Berbés** before grabbing the car. It is the seafaring neighborhood right next to the port, the city's fishing heart for centuries that still preserves that essence, with traditional taverns where you can have dinner and enjoy a quiet glass of wine. If you departed from Bueu or Portonovo, a walk around the port and dinner at a local seafood restaurant serves the exact same purpose: wrapping up the day well before the second leg.

### <span style="color: #FF7F50; font-weight: bold;">Day 2 — Pontevedra, Combarro, and sunset in Bueu</span>

Start with **Pontevedra**, take your time. Its old town is one of the best preserved in Galicia, and moving from square to square —*Praza da Leña, Praza da Ferrería, Praza da Verdura*— feels almost like a game, as each one has its own atmosphere. Along the way, check out the **Igrexa da Peregrina**, with its curious scallop-shell-shaped floor plan, one of the most recognizable images of the center. If you feel like a bite to eat, the Praza da Verdura area brings together several traditional tapas bars. Save your real appetite for what comes next.

Continue toward **Combarro** and have lunch there. Its traditional granaries (*hórreos*) are built right over the rocks, so close to the water line that at high tide they look like they're floating. After lunch, a stroll through the narrow stone streets, among stone crosses (*cruceiros*) and fishermen's houses, is a great way to digest before heading toward Bueu.

![Hórreos of Combarro in Rías Baixas by the sea](https://st5.depositphotos.com/15323148/66365/i/600/depositphotos_663659642-stock-photo-fishing-village-combarro-main-attraction.jpg)

To wind down the day, the moment you shouldn't miss: <span style="color: #FF7F50; font-weight: bold;">sunset in Tuia or Portomaior</span>. Neither beach should be rushed, so arrive with time. At Portomaior, the *Carmela* beach bar is a safe bet, with good food facing the sea. At Tuia, more sheltered and with fewer crowds, the *Chiringuito Galifornia* is famous for its mojitos. Both offer dinner service and, on some summer nights, live concerts while the sun sets over the ria. Whatever your choice, the sunset is the best way to close the day. Nothing more is needed.

## Plan 2: The lighthouse route, a well-lived stretch

The **Lighthouse Route** spans about 200 km along the **Costa da Morte**, linking lighthouses, cliffs, and fishing villages in one of the most spectacular coastal itineraries in Galicia. Doing the whole thing in a single day is not the best idea; the way to truly enjoy it on a getaway is to choose a stretch and walk it calmly.

A good starting point is the area of **Muxía and Fisterra**. The <span style="color: #FF7F50; font-weight: bold;">Fisterra Lighthouse</span>, which for centuries marked what was believed to be the end of the world, and the sanctuary of **A Barca** in Muxía, with the rocks traditionally associated with the legend of the Virgin, are must-stops on this stretch. If you prefer fewer crowds and equally impressive landscapes, the stretch toward **Cabo Vilán** in Camariñas, with its lighthouse and cliffs, is another great option.

![Faro de Cabo Vilan](https://st5.depositphotos.com/22948272/62976/i/600/depositphotos_629762390-stock-photo-lighthouse-cabo-vilan-camarinas-galicia.jpg)

Whichever stretch you choose, do it on foot. It is the best way to truly enjoy the landscape, and along the way you'll earn the feast that follows.

For lunch, in Fisterra a good option is *Restaurante O Centolo*, right by the port with sea views, specializing in local fish and seafood. If your stretch was Camariñas, by the port you'll find simple seafood restaurants with fresh catch of the day. And if you fancy something other than fish, in either village you will also find places serving good Galician beef and <span style="color: #FF7F50; font-weight: bold;">pulpo á feira</span>, the other unmissable highlight of the local gastronomy. If what you're looking for is unforgettable views, head toward **Ézaro**: the Xallas River waterfall plunges almost straight into the sea, and its terraces offer panoramas that are well worth the stop on their own.

The key to Plan 2 is not completing all 200 km, but leaving you wanting to come back next year for the next stretch.

## Plan 3: Ribeira Sacra, the other galician summer

If the first two plans taste like the sea, the **Ribeira Sacra** is the inland Galician summer: canyons carved by the Sil and Miño rivers, heroic vineyards on impossible terraces, and monasteries that have been part of the landscape for centuries.

The best way to experience the area is from the water, with a <span style="color: #FF7F50; font-weight: bold;">catamaran cruise through the Sil Canyon</span>, which lets you see from below what can only be guessed from land: rock walls over 500 meters high and vineyards clinging to almost vertical slopes. They depart from various piers, such as Doade or the Belesar recreation area, and it's wise to book in advance during peak season.

On land, two stops shape the day: the **Monastery of Santo Estevo** (now a Parador), with its cloisters from different eras and a deep, fitting silence, and a viewpoint in the area —*Cabezoás* or *Vilouxe* are great options— to see the riverbanks from above, with the vineyards drawing lines across the canyon.

![Cañón del Sil y viñedos de la Ribeira Sacra](https://st5.depositphotos.com/2071605/71639/i/600/depositphotos_716395828-stock-photo-landscape-sil-river-ribeira-sacra.jpg)

And since you are in wine country, it's worth doing a tasting at a winery featuring <span style="color: #FF7F50; font-weight: bold;">mencía or godello</span>, the two grape varieties that define the Ribeira Sacra. For lunch, *A Faragulla*, in the historic center of Chantada, is a safe recommendation: well-crafted Galician product cuisine in a simple, warm environment, perfect for wrapping up a plan as Galician and summery as the other two, even if it has very little coast.

## Three plans, one single summer

Islands with limited quotas, a lighthouse stretch to walk, and an inland canyon with its own wine. Three different ways to experience summer in Galicia, and all three fit perfectly into the same season if you know where to start.

